---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Recollection from '../components/Recollection.astro';
import EvidenceBadge from '../components/EvidenceBadge.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
---

<BaseLayout title="Larry's Story">
  <span class="eyebrow">The memoir</span>
  <h1>Larry's Story</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    What follows is Larry's year in Vietnam as he remembers it. Almost everything on this page is
    classified <EvidenceBadge level="unverified-recollection" /> — not because it is doubted, but
    because the project's work of matching memory to military records has only begun. As documents
    surface, the <a href={withBase('/research/')}>research pages</a> will record what they show.
  </p>

  <h2>Drafted, and a choice at Fort Benning</h2>
  <p>
    Larry Dennis Hunnewell was drafted into the U.S. Army in 1964. At Fort Benning, Georgia, he
    faced a fork in the road: an assignment to Turkey, or Vietnam. He volunteered for Vietnam.
  </p>

  <h2>First months: Nha Trang</h2>
  <p>
    His tour began around September 1964. He remembers spending roughly his first two months at
    <a href={withBase('/places/nha-trang/')}>Nha Trang</a>, the coastal city in south-central
    Vietnam that hosted a growing complex of American military facilities.
  </p>

  <h2>Radio operator with the 118th AOD</h2>
  <Recollection>
    He remembers his organization as the 118th AOD — Aviation Operations Detachment — a relatively
    small organization of approximately 110 men, including radio operators and pathfinders. Some
    personnel reportedly required special clearances.
  </Recollection>
  <p>
    Larry served as a radio operator, and he recalls an association with the 1st Cavalry. His
    duties involved substantial helicopter travel; he remembers moving throughout Vietnam. He
    remembers his Company Headquarters at a place he renders as
    <a href={withBase('/places/thom-son-nuht/')}>&ldquo;Thom Son Nuht&rdquo;</a> — possibly Tan
    Son Nhut, the great air base beside Saigon, though that identification is still a hypothesis.
    See <a href={withBase('/units/')}>Units</a> for what is being done to pin down the
    organization itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The Black Virgin Mountain</h2>
  <p>
    Larry remembers being on or associated with
    <a href={withBase('/places/nui-ba-den/')}>Núi Bà Đen</a>, the Black Virgin Mountain — the
    lone peak rising near Tây Ninh. He may have called it something like &ldquo;Sun
    Mountain.&rdquo; He remembers a Special Forces
    <a href={withBase('/people/sgt-lowry/')}>Sergeant Lowry or Lowery</a> who operated from its
    summit.
  </p>

  <h2>The war grows around him</h2>
  <p>
    Larry's tour spanned the year the American war changed character. In March 1965 Operation
    Rolling Thunder began the sustained bombing of North Vietnam, and the first Marine combat
    units waded ashore at Da Nang. Larry also remembers controversy or stories about aircraft
    dumping bombs into the ocean rather than attacking their intended targets — a recollection the
    project is researching carefully against documented procedures, rules of engagement, and
    period rumor.
  </p>

  <h2>Japan, August 1965: reported killed in action</h2>
  <Recollection>
    Around August 1965, Larry was in Japan. He personally remembers seeing a notice or article in
    <em>Stars and Stripes</em> saying he had been killed.
  </Recollection>
  <p>
    He was, of course, alive to read it. How his name came to appear in a casualty report — a
    clerical error, a mix-up with a similarly named soldier, something else — is the single most
    important unresolved question in this archive. A captain whose name Larry remembers as
    sounding like <a href={withBase('/people/captain-guegler/')}>&ldquo;Guegler&rdquo;</a> may be
    connected to this period.
  </p>

  <h2>Home</h2>
  <p>
    Larry's Vietnam service ended around September 1965, a year after it began — the same month
    the 1st Cavalry Division (Airmobile) was arriving in the highlands and the American war was
    becoming something far larger than the one he had joined.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sixty years on, Larry is going back through that year — looking at the
    <a href={withBase('/photos/')}>157 photographs</a> he brought home and filling in what he
    remembers. This page is the story so far, not the story finished.
  </p>

  <hr />
  <p class="record">
    Every memory above is preserved as given. Research findings are recorded separately and never
    overwrite Larry's words. <a href={withBase('/research/')}>How the archive works &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
</style>
